Durarara!! AU
Chapter 19 – Part 1
Between deliveries, Celty took the kind of break that was only ever half real.
She parked Shooter in the shadow of a side street, yellow helmet still on, PDA angled low against the handlebars while the city moved around the myth without knowing how tired the myth currently was. Courier work helped. Motion helped. What did not help was the idle second in which an old clip surfaced on a local forum mirror and refused to be scrolled past.
She had seen the night itself.
She had not really seen it.
The video was shaky, filmed on someone’s phone from the edge of a thickening crowd: a street band, an open case, and a tall masked foreigner in a gray hoodie who had stepped into the performance like he belonged to the noise. In the clip his face was hidden — black mask, sunglasses, hood up — until the second song, when the hood fell and long black braids spilled forward into the streetlight.
Celty’s gloved thumb hovered over the screen.
Then she let the first song play.
The lyrics hit differently now.
Not as anonymous edgy street performance. Not as a talented stranger showing off for a crowd. As a man she had since watched wake from a childhood forest with tears on his face. As the same voice that had talked about Justice’s broken promise, a list almost finished, cartel rooms, and the fear of everyone aging out. Lines about suicidal thoughts, about masks worn for survival, about loyalty, about people who kept someone from disappearing — all of it rearranged itself under the knowledge she now carried.
Of course.
The second song only made it worse.
Freedom. Black mask. Better late than never. The sound of someone trying to outrun a version of himself and still dragging the old name through the chorus. When the hood came down in the video and the braids fell free, the crowd in the footage reacted like they had been given a reveal. Celty reacted like she had been given a retrospective wound. She could see Rio in the memory of that night. Anri. The van gang. Herself, present and uninformed, watching a performance whose real contents she had not been equipped to hear.
On the PDA screen, the masked man sang like the words were keeping him upright.
In the present, Celty sat motionless on Shooter while black smoke tried to rise under the sealed helmet and found nowhere to go.
She had thought she understood that night as the first public fracture in his disguise — braids exposed, myth of the ordinary foreigner slipping. Now the songs themselves felt like early confessions delivered to strangers because strangers were safer than anyone who might survive the knowledge.
The clip ended on applause and the beginning of his escape into the crowd.
Celty did not replay it immediately.
She only stared at the paused frame — hood down, braids visible, mask still clinging to the lower half of a face she now knew in much closer rooms — and understood, with a low and certain discomfort, that the man who had held her that morning had been telling the truth in public long before he ever trusted her with the private version of it.
And she had missed the meaning completely the first time.
Celty remained frozen over the paused frame, yellow helmet angled down toward the PDA, and answered herself in the sealed quiet under it.
You were there.
Not later. Not secondhand. On the actual street, in the actual crowd, watching the hood fall and the braids come down like the only important reveal was identity. She had filed the night under anomaly and force and the beginning of a question about a foreigner who moved wrong for an ordinary man. She had not filed it under confession.
Of course the songs sounded like survival. They were.
All the talk of masks. Of staying undead. Of people who kept someone from going under. Of pain that needed a purpose or it would eat the person carrying it. She had heard edge and performance. What she had actually been listening to was a boy with a dead brother’s promise still stuck in his chest, throwing the truth into a public square because a crowd would not ask him to stay afterward and make it intimate.
And then you spent the next stretch of days demanding honesty from him like he had been withholding from the beginning.
The self-reproach landed clean and unwelcome. He had withheld details. The Undead structure. The white smoke. The full map. But the emotional engine — the grief, the suicidal adjacency, the loyalty, the fear of being left, the need to keep moving so the list did not end in a blank room — had been in the open air under streetlights, half-shouted over borrowed instruments while she stood close enough to feel the bass and still somehow missed the meaning.
You don’t get to call yourself attentive if you only recognize a wound after you’ve already slept beside it.
Black smoke pressed uselessly against the inside of the helmet. On the screen, the paused Curtis remained caught mid-escape, braids free, mask still on, one night away from becoming permanent in ways none of them had accounted for yet.
Celty locked the PDA.
No more watching it like a mystery clip, she told herself.
You know the man in the frame now.
Which means you also know what it cost him to sing the truth to strangers before he could stand telling it to anyone who might stay.
They were only walking.
After the café, the conversation had thinned into something quieter and almost comfortable — Anri matching Curtis’s pace through a stretch of open street, long black braids shifting against his black compression shirt as the canceled school day continued around them without urgency. For a few minutes it almost felt ordinary.
Then the crowd thickened ahead of them, and ordinary ended.
A public fight had drawn a rough ring of spectators near the mouth of a commercial side street. One fighter — clearly experienced, efficient to the point of cruelty — was already finishing a local challenger against the pavement. The challenger hit the ground hard, tried to rise, and failed. A groan moved through the onlookers. Someone laughed too loudly. Money changed hands at the edges.
Then a showman stepped into the open space, arms wide, voice pitched to carry.
“There it is! Another one done! You people keep sending neighborhood pride up against a real monster, and you keep getting the same lesson!” He swept a hand toward the winner. “Undefeated! Built different! You want a fantasy, go watch TV. You want the truth, it’s standing right here!”
The fighter was big. Very muscular. About six-one, the kind of dense, trained bulk that made ordinary street toughs look unfinished. He rolled his shoulders once and scanned the crowd with the bored confidence of a man who had already decided no one in it could correct him.
His eyes landed on Curtis and Anri.
The showman’s smile sharpened immediately.
“Ohhh — and what do we have here? Tall foreigner and a cute schoolgirl out for a stroll. You looking for directions, or did you come to test your luck?” A few people turned. The pressure of public attention swung toward them like a door. “Don’t be shy! My man still has room on the afternoon for one more disappointment!”
Anri’s body went tight beside Curtis at once.
She stepped half a fraction closer without fully meaning to, dark eyes wide under the sudden focus of the street, one hand curling near the strap of her bag as if proximity alone could make them less target-shaped.
“…Curtis-kun.” Her voice was low, strained, meant only for him. “We should go. This isn’t a normal crowd. That man is picking people out.”
A short, frightened pause as the showman kept grinning in their direction.
“Please. Don’t answer him.”
The bulky man’s mouth curled.
“Shut your mouth, bitch.”
The air shifted.
It was subtle at first — no explosion, no immediate swing — just the sudden drop in temperature around Curtis as long black braids settled against the black compression shirt and his mismatched eyes locked onto the fighter with a flatness that did not belong to a casual passerby. Anri felt it beside him at once, the way the street itself seemed to lean away from the space he occupied. A few people in the nearest arc of the crowd went quieter without knowing why.
Curtis did not raise his voice.
He simply looked at the man who had spoken to Anri like that, and for a second the undefeated posture across the ring looked less like dominance and more like a man failing to understand what he had just invited.
The showman, either missing the change or choosing to capitalize on it, lunged into the opening with a bright, greedy laugh. He dragged a large bucket into the center of the rough circle and tipped it enough for the crowd to see the thick stack of bills stuffed inside.
“Entry fee if you want to challenge!” he crowed. “You win? You get it all! Simple rules, simple money, no crying to the cops after. One shot at the monster — unless our quiet foreign friend here is only built for walking girls around and looking pretty.”
A ripple of laughter moved through part of the crowd.
Another part had already stopped laughing, eyes flicking between the bucket, the six-one undefeated fighter, and the tall man in the black compression shirt whose silence no longer felt empty.
Anri’s fingers brushed Curtis’s sleeve, barely there.
“…Curtis-kun,” she whispered again, tighter now. “He’s trying to drag you in. You don’t have to prove anything to men like that.”
Curtis had not looked away from the bulky fighter.
The money bucket sat in the street between them like bait.
The insult to Anri still hung in the air like a smell that would not clear.
Curtis sighed through his nose, long black braids shifting against the black compression shirt as he finally looked away from the bulky fighter long enough to speak to Anri.
“Wait here, Anri-chan. Some people can’t go around hurting locals or calling people names. Especially if it’s directed at you.”
Anri’s mouth opened, but he was already moving.
The crowd parted around him with the uncertain instinct of people who had just realized the tall foreigner was not being polite so much as finished choosing. Curtis walked into the rough circle and stopped in front of the showman and the undefeated monster. Up close the difference was immediate — Curtis taller by a clear margin, leaner in the fashion of dense functional muscle rather than show-bulk, braids and mismatched eyes turning him into a problem the street had not budgeted for.
He looked the bulky man over once.
“…Okay, chuckle nuts. How tough are ya?”
A few people snorted. The fighter’s jaw flexed. Before he could answer, the showman clapped both hands together like Christmas had arrived early.
“Now that’s a challenger! Tall, mean posture, actual shoulders — beautiful! But we do this like a show. Go behind that curtain and choose a song to walk out to. Atmosphere matters! Presentation matters! You want the bucket, you give the people a proper entrance!”
Curtis’s face went completely flat.
“…You’re kidding me.”
The showman beamed harder, already pointing toward a cheap curtain rigged between two temporary posts like this were a roadside arena and not a public nuisance.
“Do I look like I’m kidding? Every fighter gets a walk-out track. Builds tension. Makes the loss more memorable. Pick something with bite — unless you planned on coming in silent and disappointing everybody.”
The bulky man cracked his neck, sneering.
“Pick fast. I don’t need music to break a pretty boy in gym clothes.”
From the edge of the circle, Anri stood frozen, dark eyes wide, one hand clenched near her bag as the street around Curtis turned into theater whether he wanted the stage or not.
Behind the cheap curtain, the showman shoved a wireless mic toward Curtis like this were television.
“Name. For the announcement. Make it good.”
Curtis stared at him, long black braids resting against the black compression shirt, expression already exhausted by the theater of it.
“…It’s Curtis.”
The showman recoiled as if personally injured.
“That’s stupid. No bite. No myth. No merchandising potential.” He spun toward the gap in the curtain, already rewriting reality at full volume. “Ladies and gentlemen — from the frozen edge of the map, the braided executioner himself — the Chief of the North!”
Curtis pinched the bridge of his nose.
“…This guy is a fuckin’ showman for sure.”
He didn’t get time to argue the title. The sound system crackled, some phone-connected speaker stack roared to life, and Lump Ya Head slammed into the street at rude volume. Curtis exhaled once through his nose, pushed the curtain aside, and walked out to it anyway — tall, dark, black compression shirt locked to a fighter’s frame, long braids shifting with each step as Hollywood Undead barked threats about flapping gums and checked chins over the crowd.
The reaction was instant.
A portion of the spectators whooped on pure spectacle. Someone shouted the new nickname back. Money hands got busier at the edges. The bulky undefeated fighter squared up harder, ego flaring at the sight of a real entrance being made at his expense. For a few seconds the side street felt less like a public nuisance and more like a rogue arena that had accidentally booked someone dangerous.
Anri did not cheer.
She stood locked at the edge of the circle, dark eyes wide, one hand clenched white around the strap of her bag as Curtis walked into the open space under a name that was not his and a song that sounded far too much like a warning. The music hit hard. The crowd noise swelled. All she could see was the boy from the café — the one who had apologized for not dressing up — suddenly framed by strangers as a featured act against a man who had already put someone on the pavement.
“…Curtis-kun,” she whispered, barely audible under the track. “This is bad. This is really bad.”
No one in the ring heard her.
The Chief of the North was already in the light, and the undefeated monster was smiling like the afternoon had finally become interesting.
The bulky man came in hard.
No feeling-out jab, no patient circle — just a full rush, heavy boots tearing across the rough ring as the undefeated monster tried to turn opening pressure into an immediate statement. The crowd surged with the motion. Someone yelled. The showman howled something incoherent about first blood and destiny.
Curtis did not take his hands out of his pockets.
He only shifted his weight, long black braids sliding against the black compression shirt, mismatched eyes tracking the incoming line with clinical calm. The punch cut air where his head had been a fraction earlier. The follow-up shoulder check found nothing solid. Curtis slipped the rush by such a narrow, insulting margin that it looked less like defense and more like inspection — a man measuring speed, commitment, and balance while treating the attack as information.
When the bulky fighter overextended, Curtis’s voice landed flat and conversational over the residual noise of the track.
“…You’re as slow as you are fuckin’ stupid.”
The ring reacted all at once.
A sharp collective sound went up from the nearest arc of spectators — half shock, half delighted cruelty. Someone barked a laugh. The showman’s mouth fell open, then stretched into a greedy grin at the sudden heat in the match. The bulky man’s face flushed dark, pride cracking into raw offense as he planted his feet and swung again harder, the undefeated calm already starting to leave him.
From the edge, Anri flinched as if the insult itself might summon worse violence.
“Curtis-kun—!”
Too late for caution.
The monster of the side street had just been called slow and stupid in public, and every ugly ounce of his attention was now fixed on the taller foreigner still standing there with both hands comfortably in his pockets.
The bulky man’s face twisted.
“Scared of being hit?! Stop running, coward!”
Curtis actually laughed out loud — a short, bright, disbelieving sound that cut through the street noise more cleanly than any threat. Long black braids shifted against the black compression shirt as he finally stopped slipping the rushes and simply stood there, hands still loosely pocketed, mismatched eyes half-lidded with open disappointment.
“Look at you getting upset.”
Then he let the man hit him.
The first punch landed solid in his ribs. The second cracked across his shoulder. The third, thrown with real weight and wounded pride, smashed into his jaw hard enough that several people in the crowd winced on instinct. Curtis’s head turned with the force a fraction — and that was all. No stagger. No guarded retreat. No change in breathing. He rolled his neck once like someone checking the weather and stayed exactly where he was.
Inside the bulky man’s skull, the world refused to make sense.
I’m hitting him—
I’m hitting him clean—
Why does it feel like I’m punching steel?
His knuckles were already screaming. The undefeated monster’s arms shuddered with the feedback of impact against something that did not yield the way flesh was supposed to yield. Panic flickered under the anger, ugly and immediate.
Curtis looked almost bored.
“…You’re extremely boring. All that bravado.”
The crowd broke into noise.
Someone shouted that the foreigner was still standing. Someone else cursed and shoved closer for a better view. The showman looked ecstatically unhinged, already sensing the kind of street legend that printed free money. A few spectators who had been laughing with the bulky man earlier had gone quiet, the mood tipping from entertainment into the unsettled recognition that the wrong person had been baited into the ring.
The bulky fighter stared at his own hands, then at Curtis, chest heaving.
“What the hell are you…?”
Curtis only tilted his head, long black braids falling over one shoulder, the black compression shirt unmarked by anything that mattered.
“Disappointed,” he said. “Next.”
Anri could not move.
She stood at the edge of the rough circle with her fingers locked around the strap of her bag, dark eyes wide as the bulky man’s fists kept finding Curtis’s body and kept failing to matter. Each impact should have folded an ordinary person. Anri knew that in the abstract and still felt it wrong in the specific — the dull, heavy sound of knuckles meeting something that did not give, the way Curtis’s head only turned slightly, the complete absence of fear in a space built to manufacture it.
He’s being hit.
He’s being hit and nothing is happening.
The thought repeated with rising distress. Around her the crowd was getting louder, hungrier, delighted by the unnatural spectacle. Anri felt none of their excitement. All she felt was the cold understanding that the quiet boy from the café — the one who had apologized for not dressing up, the one who listened when people talked about disappearing into the city — was standing in public letting a violent man empty his strength against him like the violence itself was a mild inconvenience.
When Curtis called the fighter boring, something in Anri’s stomach turned.
It was not only fear for him.
It was fear of the gap between the version of Curtis who sat across from her with tired eyes and the version now occupying the ring like pain had to apply for permission to reach him.
Her voice came out thin, almost lost under the street noise.
“…Curtis-kun…”
No one in the circle heard her.
Anri took one half-step forward, then stopped, paralyzed by the knowledge that entering the space would do nothing except put her closer to a kind of power she did not understand and a kind of attention she had spent years trying not to attract. All she could do was watch, pale and rigid at the boundary of the crowd, as the undefeated monster’s confidence cracked and the foreigner in the black compression shirt remained standing like the whole performance had been a waste of his afternoon.
In her chest, one frightened conclusion formed and refused to leave:
That is not normal.
And he is still the same person who told me I didn’t have to face the city alone.
Curtis’s boredom ended in a single motion.
He drove a sharp knee up into the bulky man’s midsection hard enough to empty the air from him, then took both hands out of his pockets at last. One open palm caught the fighter by the throat. The lift that followed looked almost casual — and that was the worst part. Curtis raised the denser, shorter man clean off the pavement with a strength that made the undefeated bulk look suddenly irrelevant, long black braids shifting against the black compression shirt as mismatched eyes went flat and cold.
“Apologize to Anri-chan.”
The bulky man gagged, face darkening, pride still stupid enough to survive the lack of oxygen.
“Fuck you — and fuck that bitch—”
Curtis’s expression twisted.
Whatever restraint had kept the fight in the realm of humiliation left his face all at once. At the edge of the circle, Anri saw the change and squeezed her eyes shut, unable to watch the next second arrive. In the dark behind her eyelids she still heard it: the violent downward slam of a body hitting pavement, the ugly bounce of impact, and then the hard, final stomp that landed on the man’s lower spine with a sound the whole street felt in its teeth.
Crack.
Someone in the crowd screamed.
Someone else swore in pure shock.
The showman’s promotional grin collapsed into open-mouthed silence.
Curtis stepped off the downed fighter without looking back at the damage. He walked straight up to the showman, took the money bucket from nerveless hands, and turned away as if collecting a purchase. Long black braids hung over one shoulder. The black compression shirt was barely disturbed. Only the residual rage in his eyes still belonged to the moment before the crack.
The crowd did not cheer.
For a few stretched seconds they only stared — at the undefeated monster folded wrong on the ground, at the tall foreigner carrying the bucket, at the sudden understanding that what they had been watching was not a show that had gone too far so much as a warning that had arrived too late. A few people at the back began to leave immediately. Others remained frozen, phones half-raised, unsure whether filming would make them safer or mark them next.
From the edge of the broken circle, Anri kept her eyes shut one second longer, breath shallow, the street’s violence settling into her like cold water.
When she opened them, Curtis was already coming back toward her with the bucket in one hand and an expression that had not fully returned from wherever the rage had taken it.
Curtis did not keep the bucket.
He cut through the stunned ring to the edge where the previously beaten local challenger sat half-upright, still pale and shaken, while a girl — clearly his girlfriend — pressed a cloth to the worst of the damage and whispered furious worried things under her breath. Curtis stopped over them, long black braids shifting against the black compression shirt, and set the heavy bucket down within the man’s reach.
“Here.”
The beaten man stared at the money like it might vanish if he blinked. His girlfriend’s eyes went wide. After a second the man managed a rough, disbelieving breath.
“…Thank you. I can actually pay my rent for two months.”
Curtis only nodded once and turned away.
That was what broke the street’s silence.
The couple cheered first — raw, startled gratitude more than spectacle — and the sound cracked the fear-hold over the crowd. Someone else joined in. Then another. Within seconds the same people who had watched the spine-crack in frozen quiet were cheering the tall foreigner as he walked back across the rough circle, the narrative rewriting itself in real time from unknown danger into sudden champion with a moral bottom line. Phones came up. Someone shouted “Chief of the North!” like the showman’s joke had become scripture. The injured undefeated fighter remained on the ground, already being treated as the losing side of a story the street preferred.
Anri did not cheer.
She stood where he had left her, dark eyes open again, watching Curtis come back to her through a rising wave of public noise that made her stomach turn for reasons she could not sort cleanly. Relief was there — he was upright, returning, not lost to the violence. Gratitude too, buried deeper, for the fact that the money had gone to the man who needed rent instead of staying in Curtis’s hands as a trophy. But under both sat the image she could not erase: the twisted rage on his face, the slam, the crack, the ease of it.
When he reached her, the crowd still loud behind him, Anri’s voice came out small and unsteady.
“…Curtis-kun.”
She looked at his hands first, as if expecting them to still be shaking. They were not. That unsettled her more than if they had been.
“You helped that man,” she said, almost to convince herself of the shape of the ending. “But what you did to the other one…”
Her fingers tightened on the strap of her bag. The cheers continued around them, bright and simple in a way the moment inside her chest was not.
“…Are you okay?”
It was the only question she could ask that did not immediately become an accusation, a fear, or a confession that the boy she had called out to on a canceled school day was capable of ending a public fight like a final judgment.
Curtis stopped in front of her, long black braids shifting against the black compression shirt as the crowd noise continued to wash around them in bright, misplaced celebration. Up close, the residual heat of the fight was still in his eyes, but his voice came out careful — aimed only at her.
“I’m sorry, Anri-chan. But when he called you that name… I couldn’t just let it be.” He glanced past her toward the previously beaten local man counting the bucket with shaking hands, then back again. “Plus, he insulted the locals. That’s not right — to show up to someone’s home city and begin to insult the people that reside here.”
A short pause. His mismatched eyes lowered slightly.
“I gave him one more chance to apologize. But he couldn’t swallow his pride. I was going to throw the fight if he did apologize. But no.”
The confession sat between them under the cheering, stranger than the violence in its own way: not a man defending brutality as principle, but a man saying the worst ending had been optional until the other side refused the exit.
Anri looked up at him for a long second, dark eyes still unsettled, the crack of impact still echoing somewhere behind her thoughts. The public was already turning Curtis into a simple story. She could not.
“…You were going to lose on purpose,” she said quietly. “If he just apologized.”
It was not disbelief so much as the struggle to fit that intention against the rage she had seen twist his face. Her fingers worried the strap of her bag.
“I understand why you got angry. What he called me was—” she stopped, swallowed, then forced the rest out, “—ugly. And the way he treated people here was ugly too. I’m not saying he deserved kindness.” A shorter, tighter beat. “But the sound when you… when he hit the ground. I can’t get it out of my head yet.”
She searched his expression, looking for the café version of him inside the aftermath of the ring.
“I know you did it for a reason. I know you gave him a chance. That matters.” Her voice softened, still strained. “Please just… don’t become someone who only solves things that way. Not when you’re standing next to me. Not when I have to watch.”
The crowd kept cheering the Chief of the North.
Anri only stood there asking the actual person underneath the sudden myth not to disappear into it.
Curtis looked down.
The cheers were still going, ugly and bright around a version of the afternoon he had not asked to star in. Long black braids fell forward against the black compression shirt as the fight drained out of his posture and left something quieter and more exposed underneath.
“…I see. My apologies.”
He said it without argument. Without defending the crack in the man’s spine or the rage that had put it there. Just an acceptance of Anri’s fear, plain and immediate, as if the most important remaining fact was that she had needed to say it and he had needed to hear it.
Then he turned and began to walk away.
“Curtis-kun—?”
Anri stepped after him a second too late. The crowd, still hungry for the Chief of the North, closed around the tall foreigner with the same speed it had used to invent him. Bodies shifted. Phones rose. Someone tried to clap him on the shoulder and only caught air. By the time Anri pushed forward, the black compression shirt and long braids had already been swallowed by the moving street, absorbed into ordinary foot traffic and the residual chaos of a public fight breaking apart.
She stopped in the middle of the thinning circle, breath short, dark eyes scanning too many faces that were not his.
He was gone.
Around her, people were still talking about the foreigner who did not fall, the bucket of rent money, the undefeated monster left broken on the pavement. None of that helped. Anri stood alone in the aftermath with the sound of the spine-crack still in her memory and Curtis’s final apology still soft in her ears, realizing too late that he had taken the warning exactly as far as he knew how —
and then removed himself from her sight before she could decide whether she wanted him to stay.
A few streets away, once the crowd’s noise had thinned into ordinary city sound, Curtis finally stopped under the shadow of a side building and tipped his head back against the wall.
“…Whatever.”
The word came out low and frustrated, aimed at no one. Long black braids hung heavy against the black compression shirt as he dragged a hand down his face. He had done what felt necessary. He had given the man a chance. He had returned the money to someone who needed rent. And still Anri had looked at him like the crack in a stranger’s spine might become the shape of him if she watched too long. The apology had been real. Leaving had been real too. Better that than standing there becoming more monstrous in the eyes of someone who had only asked him to spend a canceled school day beside her.
He pushed off the wall and kept walking, jaw tight, the Chief of the North already dissolving back into just another tall foreigner on an afternoon street.

Anri did not stop searching.
She moved through the dispersing crowd with increasing urgency, dark eyes sweeping every gap between bodies, every corner, every stretch of sidewalk where a black compression shirt and long braids might reappear. The showman’s voice was already becoming memory. The injured fighter was being dealt with by other people. None of that mattered. What mattered was that Curtis had said my apologies like he accepted the fear in her voice — and then vanished before she could tell him the fear was not the same thing as rejection.
I didn’t mean leave.
The thought arrived sharp and guilty.
I meant don’t become only that. I meant stay where I can still see the person who sat across from me at the café. I meant the sound scared me, not that you were wrong to get angry when he called me that.
She cut down one street, then another, pulse high in her throat. Every tall silhouette made her step faster. Every disappointment landed harder. Around her, strangers were still replaying the fight in excited fragments — did you see the knee, he didn’t even flinch, gave the money away — turning Curtis into a clean public story while she carried the unfinished private one.
He thought I was condemning him.
That possibility sat heavier than the rest. Curtis did not argue when people drew lines near him. He apologized and removed himself. She had seen that much already in the way he carried quiet. If he had read her fear as a verdict, then he would not wait in the open for a softer revision. He would simply disappear into the city and let the distance do the rest.
Anri stopped at a crossing, breathing hard, one hand clenched around her bag strap as the light changed and the crowd moved on without him in it.
Please don’t decide that was the whole of what I felt.
But the street offered no reply, and the boy she was searching for had already chosen the harder interpretation of her silence.
Curtis found the height the way some people found silence.
The skyscraper’s upper access was not meant for the public, which made it useful. He took the last stretch of stairs without hurry, pushed out onto a narrow service ledge washed in late daylight, and sat with one knee drawn up, long black braids shifting against the black compression shirt as the city spread beneath him in hard glittering layers. Ikebukuro looked almost orderly from up there. Cleaner. Less personal.
He lit a cigarette, took the first drag, and sighed the smoke out into the open air.
The fight still sat in his hands. Not as triumph — as residue. Anri’s voice. The closed eyes. The way the crowd cheered a version of him that made the actual conversation afterward harder. He let the phone buzz once in his pocket without looking at it. Then again. Then he turned the volume down and left the ledge to the wind, the cigarette, and the temporary fiction that if he sat high enough the complications could not climb.

Below, Anri’s search had stopped looking composed.
She stood near a storefront with her phone hard in both hands, dark eyes darting from screen to street to screen again as another call failed to connect. The soft tone of his voicemail made her stomach drop every time. She switched to texts, fingers clumsy with urgency.
Anri:
Curtis-kun, where did you go?
Anri:
Please answer.
Anri:
I didn’t mean for you to leave like that.
Anri:
I’m sorry. Can you just tell me you’re okay?
No reply.
The longer the silence held, the more the afternoon rearranged itself into worst available shapes. Maybe he was hurt after all and simply had not shown it. Maybe he was angry. Maybe he had taken her fear as final and decided the cleanest kindness was disappearance. Anri paced a short, useless line along the sidewalk, then forced herself to stop, then started again. Around her the city continued, indifferent to one girl becoming frantic over a boy the street had already begun turning into a story.
She called once more.
It rang.
Rang.
Went nowhere.
Anri lowered the phone against her chest and shut her eyes, breath shallow.
Please don’t vanish on me.
High above the same ward, Curtis took another drag on the ledge and watched the smoke thin into nothing, unaware that each unanswered second was teaching Anri a different kind of height — the kind where someone could fall out of reach without ever leaving the city.
The phone lit up again on the ledge beside him.
Not Anri this time.
Celty:
I saw a video.
Street fight. Black shirt. Braids.
That was you.
A second message followed almost immediately.
Celty:
Are you hurt.
Curtis stared at the screen for a long second, cigarette burning down between his fingers, long black braids shifting in the high wind. From up here the city looked almost quiet. The messages did not. Anri’s unanswered concern. Celty’s recognition. The residual public noise of a crowd turning a moment of rage into content. All of it climbed the building after him anyway.
He let out a thin breath.
“…Nothing but more noise.”
The frustration under it was sharper than the words. He locked the phone without answering and set it face-down on the concrete, mismatched eyes narrowed against the skyline. They could want pieces. That was becoming obvious. The café listener. The rooftop confessor. The street-fight problem-solver. The man who handed rent money to a stranger and cracked another man’s spine in the same hour. Each version attracted someone. Each version also made somebody flinch.
His jaw tightened.
“None of them would truly accept all of me…”
The sentence left him into the open air with no audience but the wind. Rio’s soft claim. Anri’s frightened eyes. Celty’s complicated door. All real. All partial. All already demanding shapes of him that did not include the full, ugly, contradictory total.
Curtis tipped his head back against the building wall and watched the cigarette smoke tear apart over the drop.
“…Whatever.”
Below him, Ikebukuro kept sending messages into a phone he was no longer watching.
On the ledge, the Chief of the North sat alone with the one conclusion the height made easy and the rest of his life would eventually make expensive:
If no one could hold the whole of him, then distance was still the only structure that never asked him to cut pieces off to fit.
Curtis stayed on the ledge until the cigarette burned down to nothing useful.
Then, because height did not actually solve anything, he went back to the ground floor with his hands in his pockets and the same unfinished frustration sitting under his ribs. Long black braids shifted against the black compression shirt as he cut through the building’s lower exit and started toward home, already choosing the cleanest available option left in the day: disappear into the flat, lock the door, let the city argue with itself.
He made it ten steps from the entrance.
“Curtis-kun! Wait!”
Anri’s voice.
Curtis did not look back at once. His mouth tightened.
“…Tsk.”
The sound was quiet, aimed mostly at himself — at the fact that the one person he had walked away from had somehow still found the right stretch of street. By the time he did turn, the calm mask was already in place: shoulders loose, expression flattened into something almost easy, mismatched eyes half-lidded like the afternoon had never climbed a skyscraper or cracked a man’s spine.
“Yo.”
Anri stopped a few paces short of him, breathing hard from the chase, dark eyes bright with residual panic and relief at once. For a second she only looked at him — intact, upright, already wearing distance like a jacket — and the careful speech she might have planned fell apart on contact with the real person.
“…Don’t ‘yo’ me,” she got out, voice shaking despite the attempt at composure. “You disappeared. You weren’t answering. I thought—”
She cut herself off, fingers clenched around her bag strap, and forced the rest into something smaller and more honest.
“I wasn’t trying to drive you away. When I said what I said after the fight, I meant I was scared of the sound. Not of you choosing to protect me. Not of you giving that man a chance.” A short, strained pause. “Please don’t walk off like the only option left is vanishing. I was looking for you.”
Curtis’s calm mask slipped just enough to show the raw edge underneath. Long black braids shifted against the black compression shirt as his mismatched eyes cut away from her.
“I can’t stand when people stare at me like I’m a freak.”
Then he began to walk away again.
The words landed in Anri with delayed, awful clarity.
The closed eyes. The pale stillness at the edge of the crowd. The way she had looked at his hands when he came back, searching for tremor, for evidence, for proof that the violence had made him into something other than the boy from the café. She had told herself it was fear of the sound, fear of the crack, fear of what the city did to people — and all of that was true. But from his side of the street, it would not have looked like concern first.
It would have looked like the same stare he was naming now.
Freak.
Anri’s breath caught. She moved after him at once, not grabbing, not performing panic, just refusing to let the misunderstanding harden into another disappearance.
“Curtis-kun—wait. Please.” Her voice came out tighter, stripped of every careful distance. “You’re right. After the fight, the way I looked at you… I can see it now. I was so shaken by what happened that I stared at you like you’d become something I didn’t recognize. That was wrong.”
A short, urgent pause as she kept pace beside him.
“I wasn’t seeing a freak. I was seeing someone I care about do something terrifying in my defense, and I didn’t manage my face well enough to make that distinction clear.” Her fingers clenched in the strap of her bag. “I’m sorry. Don’t walk away from the look I should never have given you. Stay long enough for me to correct it.”
She swallowed, softer but no less direct.
“You’re not a freak to me. You weren’t then either. I was just too scared to show the rest in time.”
Curtis looked back.
The calm mask was gone. What remained was tired and defeated in a way the street fight had not managed to make him look — long black braids hanging forward against the black compression shirt, mismatched eyes dulled by more than one kind of exhaustion. For a second he only met Anri’s urgency with that quieter, heavier truth.
“…I just need to be alone.”
Anri stopped short.
The instinct to keep apologizing, to keep explaining, to physically prevent another disappearance all rose at once — and then had to make room for the plain request in front of her. He was not spitting the words as rejection. He was standing there defeated, asking for space the same way some people asked for air.
Her hands tightened on her bag strap until the knuckles paled.
“…Okay.”
The acceptance cost her. It showed in the thinness of her voice.
“If that’s what you need, I won’t chase you through the whole ward again.” A short, strained pause. “But please don’t translate alone into disappearing completely. You can need space without becoming unreachable.”
She held his gaze, dark eyes still bright with residual fear and apology.
“I’ll stop for now. I just… needed you to hear that the way I looked at you after the fight was my failure, not your definition.” Another quieter beat. “When you can stand being around someone again, I’ll still be here. That part isn’t conditional on you performing okay for me right this second.”
She took one step back, giving the request room to exist.
“…Go be alone, Curtis-kun. Just come back after.”